# What never reaches the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites: a smaller context is a
# faster build, and a context that carries `.env`, `.git` or a local virtualenv
# is a build that bakes a developer's secrets and a developer's platform into
# an image somebody else runs. `.env` in particular is the file `setup.sh`
# writes with working credentials in it.

# Secrets and local configuration
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and agent state
.git
.gitignore
.github
.claude
.idea
.vscode
# The devcontainer definition is how a laptop BUILDS a dev environment; the
# image being built here IS the environment. Shipping it would put a second,
# contradictory description of the toolchain inside the artifact it describes.
.devcontainer

# Local virtualenv and caches — the image builds its own, for its own platform
.venv
__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache

# Build output that the image regenerates
staticfiles
media
node_modules
frontend/node_modules
frontend/.next

# Documentation, method and enforcement: none of it runs in production
docs
quality
scripts/githooks
CLAUDE.md
README.md
CHANGELOG.md

# ...with ONE exception, and it is not a convenience.
#
# `apps/core/checks.py` registers the `djb` system checks unconditionally and
# reads its allow-lists from `<BASE_DIR>/quality/rules.yaml` — so with the
# whole directory excluded, EVERY `manage.py` command inside the image died on
# `djb.E000` plus a wall of E003/E013/E015/E017: `migrate` included, which
# means a deployment could not run its own release step, and `check --deploy`
# could not be shown clean where it actually matters. Found by D1, the first
# thing in this repository ever to build and run the image.
#
# So the line above is right about the *engine* and wrong about this one file.
# `quality/checks/`, `gate.py`, the canaries, the negatives and the
# measurement write-ups stay out — 1.6M of enforcement that no runtime path
# touches. `rules.yaml` is 143K of DATA that the application's own checks read,
# and it ships.
#
# THE NEGATION IS THE NARROWEST ONE THAT WORKS. Exactly one path under
# `quality/` is read at runtime (`apps/core/checks.py:61`), so exactly one path
# is un-ignored. If a second file ever becomes a runtime read, it gets its own
# line here and its own reason — never `!quality` and never a glob.
!quality/rules.yaml
# The second runtime read: `apps/core/management/commands/declare.py::product_apps` reads the
# partition's lockfile to tell a product's apps from the platform's.
!quality/partition.yaml
# The third: `apps/core/reach.py` joins a product's `scope_extensions:` onto rules 3, 11 and 16's
# lists for those same checks, so an image without the drawer refuses the product's own apps.
!quality/local/rules.yaml

# The dev-only paths `CoreConfig.DEV_ONLY` names: each reads the gate, which is not
# here; `manage.py` answers their commands with the one refusal. The census holds these lines equal.
# The scaffold copies goldens and measures with the engine's own readers.
apps/core/scaffold
# `declare` writes the drawer and validates it with the engine's rule grammar.
apps/core/management/commands/declare.py
# `newapp` records the new app's rule-91 floors through the scaffold.
apps/core/management/commands/newapp.py
# `roads` prints the census the engine's marker reader takes; it builds the scaffold's parser.
apps/core/management/commands/roads.py
# `scaffold` is the scaffold's command.
apps/core/management/commands/scaffold.py

# The compose stack is a laptop's, not a deployment's
compose.yaml
compose
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# OS noise
.DS_Store
